WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.856
To build a general solution from two solutions, they must be linearly independent.

2
00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:09.624
A convenient test is the Wronskian determinant.

3
00:00:09.624 --> 00:00:17.390
But it must be used with care: the conclusions differ for arbitrary functions and for solutions of an equation.

4
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:24.648
For two functions the Wronskian is y one times y two prime minus y one prime times y two.

5
00:00:24.648 --> 00:00:30.360
If the functions are dependent on an interval, the Wronskian vanishes identically there.

6
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.792
So if the Wronskian is nonzero at even one point, the functions are certainly independent.

7
00:00:36.792 --> 00:00:45.158
For example, for e to the x, e to the two x and e to the three x the Wronskian is two e to the six x.

8
00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:51.432
Now a cautionary example: x squared and x times the absolute value of x,

9
00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:54.624
on the interval from minus one to one.

10
00:00:54.624 --> 00:01:02.232
For positive x both functions equal x squared; for negative x the second equals minus x squared.

11
00:01:02.232 --> 00:01:07.742
In both cases the Wronskian is zero, so it vanishes identically.

12
00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:10.200
But are they dependent?

13
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.824
Suppose alpha one x squared plus alpha two x times absolute x is identically zero.

14
00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:24.288
At x equals one half, alpha one plus alpha two is zero; at x equals minus one half,

15
00:01:24.288 --> 00:01:27.744
alpha one minus alpha two is zero.

16
00:01:27.744 --> 00:01:31.992
So both alphas are zero and the functions are independent!

17
00:01:31.992 --> 00:01:38.342
Conclusion: for arbitrary functions a zero Wronskian does not imply dependence.

18
00:01:38.592 --> 00:01:42.072
For solutions the situation is different.

19
00:01:42.072 --> 00:01:48.504
If y one and y two solve one linear homogeneous equation with continuous coefficients,

20
00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:52.416
the Wronskian is either zero everywhere or nowhere.

21
00:01:52.416 --> 00:02:00.432
The Abel–Liouville formula shows it: W equals W at the starting point times e to the minus integral of p,

22
00:02:00.432 --> 00:02:03.998
and an exponential is never zero.

23
00:02:04.248 --> 00:02:08.784
The graph shows x squared and x times absolute x.

24
00:02:08.784 --> 00:02:13.464
On the right they coincide, on the left they are mirror images.

25
00:02:13.464 --> 00:02:19.622
No single constant factor works on both sides, so the functions are independent.

26
00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:25.680
n independent solutions of an n-th order equation form a fundamental set.

27
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.768
Every solution of the homogeneous equation is their linear combination.

28
00:02:30.768 --> 00:02:37.800
The general solution of the nonhomogeneous equation is that combination plus one particular solution.

29
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:45.398
For y double prime plus y equals x it is C one cos x plus C two sin x plus x.

30
00:02:45.648 --> 00:02:52.176
Check yourself: what can be said if the Wronskian of two arbitrary functions is identically zero?

31
00:02:52.176 --> 00:02:54.566
Choose an answer.
